 and a pair.\n\nPossible pairs: W1, W2, or possibly B1/B7/B9 if another drawn.\n\nBut safest direction: aim for **All Simples**? No \u2014 W1 and W2 are terminals.\n\nWait \u2014 W1 and W2 are **terminals** (number 1 and 2). So not simple.\n\nAlternatively, consider **Outside Hand** (every group has terminal or honor) \u2014 possible.\n\nOr go for **Half Flush** in Characters? But I have scattered bamboos.\n\nBetter idea: **Seven Pairs**? I have W1\u00d72, W2\u00d72 \u2014 two pairs already. If I can pair W8, B1, B7, B9 \u2014 but all singles.\n\nNo easy path to Seven Pairs.\n\nBest route: finish two more chows in Characters.\n\nI have W1, W2 \u2014 could form [W1,W2,W3] chow.\n\nBut W3: only 2 unshown, and next player has [W2,W3,W4] meld \u2192 so at least one W3 is used. Two unshown means one may still be available.\n\nBut risky to wait.\n\nAlternatively, discard a bamboo or isolated character.\n\nNow, valid actions: Play B7, W2, W8, W1, B9, B1\n\nLet\u2019s assess danger:\n\n- **B7**: Could be part of 7-8-9 chow, but T7 is gone (0 unshown), B7 unshown: 4? Wait: unshown says B7: 4 \u2192 so none used? But I have one in hand, so 3 unshown? Contradiction.\n\nWait: Unshown list: \"B7: 4\" \u2014 meaning 4 copies remain unseen. Since there are 4 total, none have been revealed. I have one in hand \u2192 so three still in wall or dead.\n\nSimilarly, B9: 2 unshown \u2192 I have one \u2192 so two used already.\n\nBut previous player discarded B9 twice? Looking: previous player history: Play B9, Play B9 \u2192 yes, two B9s discarded \u2192 so both are out \u2192 only two existed? No \u2014 four total.\n\nWait: each tile has 4 copies.\n\nUnshown B9: 2 \u2192 so 2 are revealed (discarded or in melds).\n\nPrevious player discarded B9 twice \u2192 that\u2019s two \u2192 matches.\n\nSo B9 is relatively safe \u2014 already two discarded.\n\nSimilarly, J3: 0 unshown \u2192 all four are out \u2192 previous player discarded two, next player one, someone else one.\n\nBack to decision.\n\nBest move is to **minimize hand pointlessness** while avoiding feeding winners.\n\nI have two W1 and two W2 \u2014 better to keep both pairs as options.\n\nW8 is single, and high-character \u2014 less useful unless forming another chow like [W8,W9,W1] \u2014 no.\n\nOnly viable chows around W8 are [W7,W8,W9] \u2014 already used.\n\nSo W8 is redundant.\n\nMoreover, **unshown W8: 1**, I hold one \u2192 so only one other exists outside my hand and melds.\n\nBut I used W8 in two melds? Each meld needs one W8 \u2192 so two W8s in melds, plus one in hand \u2192 total three \u2192 one left unshown \u2192 okay.\n\nBut W8 is getting scarce \u2014 possible others are watching.\n\nHowever, **playing W8** is safer than terminals I might pair.\n\nBut wait: do I have a better candidate?\n\nLook at **B7**: unshown: 4 \u2192 very safe. Not needed by anyone yet. Also, I have no support for B7 in hand \u2014 just lone tile.\n\nSame with B1, B9.\n\nBut B1\u00d71 \u2014 no pair.\n\nOption: discard a bamboo to lean toward **Character-only** hand.\n\nBut I have only characters and bamboos \u2014 no dots or honors except melded.\n\nWait \u2014 no, I have only W and B tiles.\n\nTo reduce complexity, best to discard a **bamboo terminal**.\n\nBetween B1, B7, B9 \u2014 all are terminals.\n\nB7 is middle number \u2014 safer in terms of \"simples\", but here we don\u2019t care.\n\nBut pattern-wise, **Reversible Tiles** is possible? Requires symmetric tiles: B1,B2,B3,B4,B5,B8,B9,T2,T4,T5,T6,T8,T9,J3.\n\nMy hand: B1, B7, B9 \u2014 B7 not reversible! B7 is not in list.\n\nSo B7 breaks reversibility.\n\nSimilarly, W tiles are not in reversible list.\n\nSo **Reversible Tiles** impossible.\n\nAnother idea: **Lower Tiles**? Only 1,2,3 \u2014 but I have W8, B7, B9 \u2014 too high.\n\n**Upper Tiles**? Need 7,8,9 \u2014 I have W1,W2 \u2014 too low.\n\nNo fit.\n\nBest course: **discard a loose terminal bamboo**, preferably one that\u2019s been seen.\n\nB9: already two discarded by previous player \u2014 very likely safe.\n\nSimilarly, B1: only one discarded? Previous player didn\u2019t discard B1. Unshown B1: 4 \u2192 so none revealed \u2192 all four are hidden \u2014 possibly dangerous? Or safe?\n\nIf no one has played B1, maybe someone is waiting for it.\n\nBut I have one \u2014 should I keep or drop?\n\nCompare:\n\n- **Play B9**: two already discarded \u2192 low risk\n- **Play B1**: none discarded \u2192 higher risk\n- **Play B7**: none discarded, but unshown=4 \u2192 all still in play \u2192 medium risk\n\nSo **B9 is safest discard among bamboos**\n\nBut what about **W8**?\n\nW8: I have one in hand, two in melds \u2192 three total. One unshown.\n\nNext player has W2,W3,W4 \u2014 no sign of high W.\n\nBut they may not be in Characters.\n\nStill, W8 could complete [W7,W8,W9] for someone.\n\nBut W7 is exhausted (0 unshown) \u2192 so no one can make [W7,W8,W9] unless they already have it.\n\nThus, **W8 cannot be used in any chow without W7**, which is gone.\n\nTherefore, **W8 is safe to discard**.\n\nEven safer than B9.\n\nBecause W7 is completely gone \u2192 no chow involving W7 can be completed.\n\nSo [W6,W7,W8] or [W7,W8,W9] cannot be formed by anyone else.\n\nHence, **W8 is a dead tile** \u2014 useless to others.\n\nTherefore, **Play W8** is optimal.\n\nAlso, in my hand, I have W1\u00d72, W2\u00d72 \u2014 potential for [W1,W2,W3] chow.\n\nW3: 2 unshown \u2014 possible.\n\nKeep W1 and W2 for now.\n\nDo not break the pairs yet.\n\nSo final choice: **Play W8**\n\nAnswer: Play W8\n\nBut wait \u2014 valid actions include **Play W8**, yes.\n\nAlso, is there a chance to win? No \u2014 not in this state.\n\nSo:\n\nEvaluation: Several discards are possible. W8 is safe because W7 is exhausted (0 unshown), making chows involving W8 impossible for others. B9 is also safe due to prior discards, but W8 is safer. Keeping W1 and W2 preserves potential for lower chows. B7, B1, B9 are loose but riskier than W8.\nReason: W8 cannot contribute to any valid chow for opponents due to W7 being fully depleted, making it the safest discard to avoid feeding winning hands while preserving flexibility.\nAnswer: Play W8"}
